### 12. Liquid Clustering vs Partitioning vs Z-ORDER

| Aspect | PARTITION BY | Liquid Clustering | Z-ORDER |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Syntax** | `PARTITIONED BY (col)` | `CLUSTER BY (col1, col2)` | `OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY (col)` |
| **When set** | At CREATE TABLE time — cannot change | At CREATE TABLE or ALTER — can change anytime | On-demand via OPTIMIZE |
| **How it works** | Creates physical directories per partition value | Dynamically regroups data within files using Hilbert curves | Interleaves sort order within files |
| **Best for** | Low-cardinality columns (date, region) | Any cardinality, multi-column filtering | Ad-hoc optimization of existing tables |
| **Small file problem** | YES — high cardinality = millions of tiny files | NO — files stay \~1 GB | NO — only rewrites existing files |
| **Filter performance** | Excellent if query filters on partition column | Excellent for clustered columns | Good but degrades with many Z-ORDER columns |
| **Can change columns** | NO — requires table rebuild | YES — `ALTER TABLE ... CLUSTER BY (new_col)` | YES — just re-run OPTIMIZE |
| **Maintenance** | None needed | `OPTIMIZE table` periodically | `OPTIMIZE table ZORDER BY (cols)` periodically |

> **Recommendation**: Prefer **Liquid Clustering** for new tables. Use **PARTITION BY** only for low-cardinality date columns that are always in your WHERE clause.

